{
  "image_name": "3def2a6451e9a4ffde4b9661fb790e47.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how signals from the tumour microenvironment drive epithelial–mesenchymal transition (EMT) in a tumour cell through many converging pathways. Surrounding stromal and immune cells, including macrophages, lymphocytes, and fibroblasts, provide growth factors and cytokines such as TGFβ, IL-6, EGF, FGF, Hedgehog, and WNT that engage ligand–receptor interactions at the tumour cell surface. These inputs activate major intracellular signalling modules, including RAS–MAPK and PI3K–AKT–mTOR, as well as ROS, FAK–SRC, and mechanosensing pathways linked to integrins and an abnormal extracellular matrix.\n\nThe diagram also shows additional environmental influences, including hypoxia/low pH, nutrient stress, immune responses, chemotherapy, and radiation, many of which appear to act through mitochondria, ROS, or matrix-related signalling. BMP signalling, YAP/TAZ at tight junctions, AMPK under low-energy conditions, and microRNAs are included as further regulators that can either promote or oppose EMT.\n\nAll of these pathways converge on the nucleus, where EMT-associated transcription factors such as SNAIL, ZEB, TWIST, STAT3, β-catenin, NF-κB, and HIF1α induce EMT genes. Overall, the image emphasizes EMT as a network-level response integrating biochemical, metabolic, mechanical, and therapeutic cues.",
